WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.734 --> 00:00:03.069
-Our first guest tonight is
a multi-Grammy-Award winning

00:00:03.169 --> 00:00:05.170
musician whose song,
"No Time To Die"

00:00:05.271 --> 00:00:07.440
was recently nominated
for an Academy Award.

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:10.777
She is making history as the
youngest artist to headline

00:00:10.877 --> 00:00:13.813
both the Glastonbury
and Coachella music festivals

00:00:13.913 --> 00:00:15.348
this year, and is currently on

00:00:15.448 --> 00:00:17.216
her "Happier Than Ever"
world tour.

00:00:17.316 --> 00:00:19.319
Please welcome to the show,
Billie Eilish, everybody.

00:00:19.419 --> 00:00:20.987
[ cheers and applause ]

00:00:21.087 --> 00:00:26.125
  

00:00:26.225 --> 00:00:31.230
  

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:36.369
  

00:00:36.469 --> 00:00:39.271
-Welcome to the show, Billie!
-Thank you. How you doing?

00:00:39.372 --> 00:00:41.774
-I'm fantastic.
I'm so happy to have you here.

00:00:41.874 --> 00:00:43.776
And New York has been
so happy to have you here.

00:00:43.876 --> 00:00:45.845
You got to play
Madison Square Garden.

00:00:45.945 --> 00:00:47.647
-I sure did.

00:00:47.747 --> 00:00:50.082
[ cheers and applause ]

00:00:50.182 --> 00:00:52.551
-I think all of us are in awe

00:00:52.651 --> 00:00:56.722
at how much you've accomplished
in your very young life so far.

00:00:56.822 --> 00:00:59.191
But I can imagine,
does it still blow your mind,

00:00:59.291 --> 00:01:01.093
when you're in a room like MSG?

00:01:01.193 --> 00:01:02.896
-Yes.
-That's great.

00:01:02.996 --> 00:01:04.597
[ audience awws ]
-Yeah, no, it's great.

00:01:04.697 --> 00:01:06.733
I did bawl onstage.
-Okay, gotcha.

00:01:06.833 --> 00:01:09.268
I did.

00:01:09.368 --> 00:01:10.737
Yeah, it was just really weird.

00:01:10.837 --> 00:01:12.104
I have a song during the set

00:01:12.204 --> 00:01:14.173
where it's
all baby footage of me.

00:01:14.273 --> 00:01:15.274
[ audience awws ]

00:01:15.374 --> 00:01:17.376
And I was like,
walking across the stage

00:01:17.476 --> 00:01:19.979
looking at baby me,
looking at the crowd,

00:01:20.079 --> 00:01:21.280
and I bawled.

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:22.581
-Yeah.
-Quite embarrassing.

00:01:22.681 --> 00:01:23.616
But what are you gonna do?
-What are you gonna do?

00:01:23.716 --> 00:01:25.050
I will say, and again,

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:26.619
I think the reaction
from this audience

00:01:26.719 --> 00:01:27.787
bears that out --

00:01:27.887 --> 00:01:29.622
I don't think
your audience minds

00:01:29.722 --> 00:01:30.857
when you get a little emotional.

00:01:30.957 --> 00:01:32.024
-No, they love it.
-Yeah, I think they do, too.

00:01:32.124 --> 00:01:35.961
[ cheering ]

00:01:36.062 --> 00:01:38.230
And this strikes me as
a relationship you had

00:01:38.330 --> 00:01:41.534
with your live audience
even before this pandemic

00:01:41.634 --> 00:01:42.935
that took it away from us,
right?

00:01:43.035 --> 00:01:45.471
You always had a very close
relationship with your audience,

00:01:45.571 --> 00:01:46.739
and one that was very
honest and open.

00:01:46.839 --> 00:01:48.708
And you always told them
how much they meant to you.

00:01:48.808 --> 00:01:50.142
-Yeah.

00:01:50.242 --> 00:01:56.282
I mean, the fan connection has
been the number one priority

00:01:56.382 --> 00:01:57.549
since literally the beginning.

00:01:57.650 --> 00:01:59.852
I wouldn't want to do
any of this without it.

00:01:59.952 --> 00:02:01.287
I'm dead serious.

00:02:01.387 --> 00:02:04.757
There's no reason to do any of
this without the connection.

00:02:04.857 --> 00:02:06.025
Like,
literally no reason at all.

00:02:06.125 --> 00:02:07.794
-We love you, Billie!

00:02:07.894 --> 00:02:11.097
[ applause ]

00:02:11.197 --> 00:02:12.331
-They write a lot of signs.

00:02:12.431 --> 00:02:13.766
There's a lot of preparation
that goes into

00:02:13.866 --> 00:02:14.934
going to see you live.

00:02:15.034 --> 00:02:16.936
And I think that speaks to
the connection.

00:02:17.036 --> 00:02:18.237
-It's ridiculous.

00:02:18.337 --> 00:02:19.505
-This makes sense.

00:02:19.605 --> 00:02:21.340
I didn't realize this until
I heard you say it.

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:25.011
It must be hard to read a sign
while you're singing a song.

00:02:25.111 --> 00:02:26.311
-I can't do it.
-Okay.

00:02:26.411 --> 00:02:27.446
-I literally can't do it.

00:02:27.546 --> 00:02:30.182
I try to, because I want
to read their signs.

00:02:30.282 --> 00:02:33.018
They work so hard on them.
They color them in and draw.

00:02:33.118 --> 00:02:36.689
I -- I -- Yeah.

00:02:36.789 --> 00:02:39.091
I'll be like, "Oh, cool sign,"
and I'll read it,

00:02:39.191 --> 00:02:41.060
and then I forget the words,
and it's really bad.

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:42.728
-You wouldn't want to
accidentally

00:02:42.828 --> 00:02:43.796
sing the words on the sign,
sometime.

00:02:43.896 --> 00:02:45.197
-No. No.

00:02:45.297 --> 00:02:46.599
-This one, you know.

00:02:46.699 --> 00:02:48.501
That is somebody who wants, so,

00:02:48.601 --> 00:02:51.303
"Sign my body and I will
tattoo it on me."

00:02:51.404 --> 00:02:53.372
They want you to sign it
and they would take --

00:02:53.472 --> 00:02:55.408
-That's crazy pressure, man.
Right in the middle of a show?

00:02:55.508 --> 00:02:56.609
Please.
-This one, though.

00:02:56.709 --> 00:02:57.977
These are really...

00:02:58.077 --> 00:02:59.145
These are a little bit
harder to read,

00:02:59.245 --> 00:03:00.112
but I've read them earlier.

00:03:00.213 --> 00:03:02.281
So this one --
-[ laughing ]

00:03:02.381 --> 00:03:05.651
-"Will you pierce my nipples?"

00:03:05.751 --> 00:03:07.820
[ laughter ]

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:10.156
-Can you imagine if I was like,
"Yeah, let's do it right now."

00:03:10.256 --> 00:03:11.691
-"Yeah, let's do it right now."

00:03:11.791 --> 00:03:13.359
And then then this one is even,
this is,

00:03:13.459 --> 00:03:15.395
"Burp into the mic."

00:03:15.495 --> 00:03:18.698
And by the way, I think they
just printed these out.

00:03:18.798 --> 00:03:19.799
They didn't even work
that hard on them.

00:03:19.899 --> 00:03:21.267
-No, that's true,
but the crazy thing is,

00:03:21.367 --> 00:03:24.870
they hold those up literally
for the entire hour and a half.

00:03:24.970 --> 00:03:25.904
They never put them down.

00:03:26.004 --> 00:03:27.740
And I'm all like,
"Burp into the mic"?

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:31.343
Like, okay. That's
the statement you want to be...

00:03:31.443 --> 00:03:32.911
-Also, it's a very hard thing.
-I love it.

00:03:33.012 --> 00:03:35.848
-You want to tell them, like,
"If I do that,

00:03:35.948 --> 00:03:37.349
no one here will understand

00:03:37.449 --> 00:03:39.018
it's 'cause it was on
your weird, little sign."

00:03:39.118 --> 00:03:40.519
[ laughter ]

00:03:40.619 --> 00:03:42.255
And then sometimes
they collectively,

00:03:42.355 --> 00:03:43.956
like, a large group of them
make signs.

00:03:44.056 --> 00:03:45.257
[ audience awws ]

00:03:45.357 --> 00:03:46.659
That's is really nice.
-This makes me cry.

00:03:46.759 --> 00:03:48.527
-I mean, look at these faces.
-Yeah.

00:03:48.627 --> 00:03:50.329
-They do that.

00:03:50.429 --> 00:03:53.700
I think that what they do
is somebody organizes it

00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:55.935
and they tell everybody else,
and then they're all like,

00:03:56.035 --> 00:03:57.237
"Okay, here's all these signs."

00:03:57.337 --> 00:03:59.805
I'm guessing that they make,
like, a bunch at once

00:03:59.905 --> 00:04:01.040
and then they hand them out
and they're like,

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:02.575
"For this song,
let's hold them up."

00:04:02.675 --> 00:04:03.742
That's what I thought.

00:04:03.842 --> 00:04:06.445
It's so cute.
I have cried for that, too.

00:04:06.545 --> 00:04:08.514
-And this festival, I mean,

00:04:08.614 --> 00:04:10.116
doing festivals again
must be so exciting,

00:04:10.216 --> 00:04:11.417
and headlining these big...

00:04:11.517 --> 00:04:14.120
How excited are you about
getting back and doing that?

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:15.288
-Oh, my God, so good.

00:04:15.388 --> 00:04:17.557
I did a few in September,
and those were like,

00:04:17.657 --> 00:04:19.892
the first back since COVID.

00:04:19.992 --> 00:04:20.960
[ applause ]

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:22.762
Oh, it feels so good.

00:04:22.862 --> 00:04:27.633
-That, especially that time,
obviously another wave came

00:04:27.733 --> 00:04:29.168
and made it a little bit
less enthusiastic,

00:04:29.268 --> 00:04:30.503
being out in public,

00:04:30.603 --> 00:04:34.240
but that late summer, early fall
was an exceptional time

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:36.075
to be doing
live performing again.

00:04:36.175 --> 00:04:37.643
-Yeah, oh, my God,
we were free.

00:04:37.743 --> 00:04:38.945
It was great.

00:04:39.045 --> 00:04:41.113
It was like, a month of like,
"Oh, COVID's gone."

00:04:41.213 --> 00:04:42.815
Nope.

00:04:42.915 --> 00:04:45.318
-And then you went
and you did "SNL."

00:04:45.418 --> 00:04:47.019
You hosted "SNL."
-I did.

00:04:47.119 --> 00:04:50.489
[ applause ]

00:04:50.589 --> 00:04:53.393
-And like, just got in under the
wire before COVID came back.

00:04:53.493 --> 00:04:55.227
-Literally the week before.
-Yeah.

00:04:55.327 --> 00:04:58.497
-I think the host the week after
you left was omicron.

00:04:58.597 --> 00:04:59.965
-Uh-huh. It was.

00:05:00.065 --> 00:05:01.634
It was.
-It was omicron.

00:05:01.734 --> 00:05:03.502
I have a bunch of questions
about "SNL."

00:05:03.602 --> 00:05:04.904
Stick around,
we will be right back

00:05:05.004 --> 00:05:08.373
with more from Billie Eilish.

00:05:08.473 --> 00:05:10.776
-Welcome back to "Late Night."
We're here with Billie Eilish.

00:05:10.877 --> 00:05:12.177
And I --
[ cheers and applause ]

00:05:12.277 --> 00:05:13.479
I do want to
ask about "SNL,"

00:05:13.579 --> 00:05:16.482
but congratulations on your
Oscar nomination.

00:05:16.582 --> 00:05:18.951
-Oh, my gosh!
[ cheers and applause ]

00:05:19.051 --> 00:05:20.319
-For your Bond song.
-I know.

00:05:20.419 --> 00:05:22.888
And even being asked to do '
a James Bond song --

00:05:22.988 --> 00:05:24.223
it's such a unique thing.

00:05:24.323 --> 00:05:27.226
There's no other kind of
theme song where, you know,

00:05:27.326 --> 00:05:28.894
through the years,
it's all these iconic musicians

00:05:28.994 --> 00:05:30.262
have taken it on.

00:05:30.362 --> 00:05:33.399
What was that process like
when you got asked to do it?

00:05:33.499 --> 00:05:37.570
Like, who did you have to
meet with in the writing?

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:42.575
-Well, it was -- We made
the song in October of 2019.

00:05:42.675 --> 00:05:43.842
So this was
a long, long time ago.

00:05:43.942 --> 00:05:45.544
-Yeah, that's a long time.
-It's a long time ago.

00:05:45.644 --> 00:05:46.546
-That was a movie that got --
Okay.

00:05:46.646 --> 00:05:49.348
-So, 17 when we made it.
I'm now 20.

00:05:49.448 --> 00:05:50.316
-Yeah, there you go.
-Interesting.

00:05:50.416 --> 00:05:53.486
-Yeah.
-Very, very weird.

00:05:53.586 --> 00:05:57.923
Yeah, it was a, like, very long
kind of strategic process.

00:05:58.024 --> 00:06:00.025
It wasn't like,
"You got the job, here it is."

00:06:00.125 --> 00:06:03.262
We were auditioning,
pretty much.

00:06:03.362 --> 00:06:05.798
So it was it was not,
like, an ego thing.

00:06:05.898 --> 00:06:07.599
It was like, really,
it's got to be perfect.

00:06:07.699 --> 00:06:08.968
We've got to do it right.

00:06:09.068 --> 00:06:11.070
They weren't like, "Oh, yeah,
we want Billie Eilish."

00:06:11.170 --> 00:06:14.507
It was like, "We want you,"
and we auditioned

00:06:14.607 --> 00:06:18.344
and kind of just, like, did our
best and worked really hard.

00:06:18.444 --> 00:06:21.914
And it was a lot of, like,
Barbara, and she kind of gave us

00:06:22.014 --> 00:06:23.983
the first little bit of
the script,

00:06:24.083 --> 00:06:25.451
like, just, like, the opening,

00:06:25.551 --> 00:06:27.186
just so that we had a little,
like, taste of the movie.

00:06:27.286 --> 00:06:29.488
-Was that helpful
in the writing of the song?

00:06:29.588 --> 00:06:30.989
-It was literally
what wrote the song.

00:06:31.089 --> 00:06:33.259
-That's wonderful.
-Yeah. It was great.

00:06:33.359 --> 00:06:35.027
-And you got to meet with
Daniel Craig.

00:06:35.127 --> 00:06:36.195
-Yeah.
-Were you nervous when you --

00:06:36.295 --> 00:06:37.462
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:06:37.563 --> 00:06:40.099
-Yeah. It's James Bond.
-Yeah, he's James Bond.

00:06:40.199 --> 00:06:42.034
-He's James Bond.
-And --

00:06:42.134 --> 00:06:43.102
-He's a DILF.

00:06:43.202 --> 00:06:44.737
-He is a DILF.
[ laughter ]

00:06:44.837 --> 00:06:46.038
Thank you for saying it.

00:06:46.138 --> 00:06:51.010
I found, when I say it,
it's weird,

00:06:51.110 --> 00:06:53.879
but I want you to know that
I am in full agreement with you.

00:06:53.979 --> 00:06:54.947
-You should be.

00:06:55.047 --> 00:06:56.315
-But I didn't say it.
I just agreed.

00:06:56.415 --> 00:06:57.749
-Those eyes? Girl...
-Yeah.

00:06:57.849 --> 00:07:00.920
I mean, those eyes are crazy.
Look, let me just say something.

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:02.688
You have a beautiful blue eyes.
I have beautiful blue eyes.

00:07:02.788 --> 00:07:04.123
-You do. You absolutely do.

00:07:04.223 --> 00:07:08.126
-But we can't even be in the
same room as that man's eyes.

00:07:08.226 --> 00:07:09.428
-No, no, no, no. No.

00:07:09.528 --> 00:07:12.765
-These look like pinkeye
compared to him.

00:07:12.865 --> 00:07:14.400
-Literally,
you would not believe them.

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:17.270
Like, they look crazy.
-They are crazy.

00:07:17.370 --> 00:07:19.005
-When I met him,
I was like, "Whoa!"

00:07:19.105 --> 00:07:21.773
Like, they're, like -- It feels
like they're here on him.

00:07:21.874 --> 00:07:23.042
Like, this is his face,
and these are his eyes.

00:07:23.142 --> 00:07:25.478
-Exactly.
Like, he's -- You want --

00:07:25.578 --> 00:07:27.346
If you weren't psyched about it,
you'd say,

00:07:27.446 --> 00:07:28.581
"Your eyes are
in my personal space."

00:07:28.681 --> 00:07:30.415
-Yeah, yeah, you would!

00:07:30.516 --> 00:07:32.451
-The first time he looked at me,
I went, "DILF!"

00:07:32.551 --> 00:07:35.688
I didn't --
couldn't stop myself.

00:07:35.788 --> 00:07:38.024
-You should get on that.
-Well, I don't know.

00:07:38.124 --> 00:07:39.425
-Explore.
-I don't know.

00:07:39.525 --> 00:07:41.160
I do feel like it would be
weird if my wife said,

00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:42.928
"You get one hall pass,"
and I was like, "Daniel Craig."

00:07:43.028 --> 00:07:44.496
-I don't think it
would be weird.

00:07:44.596 --> 00:07:45.631
I think you should do it.

00:07:45.731 --> 00:07:47.165
-Thank you.
-Do you have a hall pass?

00:07:47.265 --> 00:07:48.266
-No.

00:07:48.367 --> 00:07:49.735
-Daniel Craig.
-What? Yeah, no.

00:07:49.835 --> 00:07:53.172
Well, I'm going to have to go
talk to her about it.

00:07:53.272 --> 00:07:56.275
You did a wonderful --
You and Finneas

00:07:56.375 --> 00:07:57.376
are going to go
to the Oscars together.

00:07:57.476 --> 00:07:58.577
-Yeah.
-That's really cool.

00:07:58.677 --> 00:07:59.578
-Yeah.

00:07:59.678 --> 00:08:02.615
-Is there anything in particular

00:08:02.715 --> 00:08:03.950
you're most excited about
the night?

00:08:04.050 --> 00:08:04.984
Will you be nervous
the whole day, being a nominee?

00:08:05.084 --> 00:08:06.752
-Oh, my God,
it's the scariest thing.

00:08:06.852 --> 00:08:08.821
No, I went to
the Oscars in 2020,

00:08:08.921 --> 00:08:10.522
right before COVID happened,
and it was, like,

00:08:10.622 --> 00:08:12.858
the coolest,
scariest thing I'd ever --

00:08:12.958 --> 00:08:14.760
I've never been more nervous
in my life.

00:08:14.860 --> 00:08:16.896
"SNL" and the Oscars are, like,

00:08:16.996 --> 00:08:18.764
the scariest thing
I've ever experienced.

00:08:18.864 --> 00:08:21.467
And -- 'Cause it's not people
that I know, really.

00:08:21.567 --> 00:08:22.468
It's actors.

00:08:22.568 --> 00:08:24.303
And, also, actors are so

00:08:24.403 --> 00:08:26.138
much more eloquent
than musicians.

00:08:26.238 --> 00:08:28.307
Like, musicians are, like,
weird and gross and smelly,

00:08:28.407 --> 00:08:31.877
and actors are, like, on time
and very -- I don't know.

00:08:31.977 --> 00:08:33.345
So it was intimidating.

00:08:33.445 --> 00:08:35.381
I was --
Yeah, so I'm very nervous.

00:08:35.481 --> 00:08:38.317
-If it goes anywhere
as well as your "SNL,"

00:08:38.417 --> 00:08:39.385
you have nothing to worry about.

00:08:39.485 --> 00:08:40.986
This was --
A lot of things I loved.

00:08:41.086 --> 00:08:44.323
This was my personal favorite.
[ cheers and applause ]

00:08:44.423 --> 00:08:45.758
You and Kate
did this sketch together.

00:08:45.858 --> 00:08:47.025
-Yeah.

00:08:47.125 --> 00:08:49.762
-And this is very good
still photo, because it --

00:08:49.862 --> 00:08:52.598
You never fully broke,
but it did seem like

00:08:52.698 --> 00:08:54.900
that was Kate's goal.

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:57.236
-Yeah.
We had trouble with this one.

00:08:57.336 --> 00:08:58.738
We -- Yeah.

00:08:58.838 --> 00:09:03.342
Well, you know, in the dress
rehearsal, I was tearing up.

00:09:03.442 --> 00:09:08.547
I was dying laughing and I felt
like I was going to explode.

00:09:08.647 --> 00:09:10.716
It was really
honestly genuinely painful.

00:09:10.816 --> 00:09:12.284
Like, you know when
you're not supposed to laugh

00:09:12.384 --> 00:09:13.385
and you have to laugh?

00:09:13.485 --> 00:09:14.920
It's -- It hurts.
It genuinely hurts.

00:09:15.020 --> 00:09:16.522
-Also, watching it
and knowing the show,

00:09:16.622 --> 00:09:17.756
there was no release point.

00:09:17.856 --> 00:09:19.692
You guys were on camera
the whole time.

00:09:19.792 --> 00:09:21.193
There wasn't a time where it
cut away where you could, like,

00:09:21.293 --> 00:09:22.194
put yourself back together.

00:09:22.294 --> 00:09:23.195
-I know.

00:09:23.295 --> 00:09:26.365
And she just was
doing so many things

00:09:26.465 --> 00:09:28.300
that were pushing me
over the edge.

00:09:28.400 --> 00:09:29.701
Oh, my God, it was infuriating.

00:09:29.802 --> 00:09:32.171
-And your your brother had
a fantastic cameo

00:09:32.271 --> 00:09:33.238
in the sketch, as well.

00:09:33.338 --> 00:09:34.373
-Yeah.
-Now, was he --

00:09:34.473 --> 00:09:35.807
[ cheers and applause ]
-Yeah, it is.

00:09:35.907 --> 00:09:38.744
-Is he a hard sell on that
when they asked him to do it

00:09:38.844 --> 00:09:40.312
or was he immediately like,
"I'm in"?

00:09:40.412 --> 00:09:41.747
-Oh, he was in for sure.
-Yeah. He was great.

00:09:41.847 --> 00:09:43.749
-It's "SNL"!
-Yeah, exactly.

00:09:43.849 --> 00:09:45.851
-Want a cameo, you get a cameo.

00:09:45.951 --> 00:09:48.888
-I want to finish by asking --
Tell everybody what this is.

00:09:48.988 --> 00:09:51.123
[ cheers and applause ]
They seem to know.

00:09:51.223 --> 00:09:52.891
They seem to know.
-Okay.

00:09:52.991 --> 00:09:56.329
-So, this is my fragrance.

00:09:56.429 --> 00:10:01.600
It's called Eilish,
and it's just --

00:10:01.700 --> 00:10:03.268
It speaks for itself.
-Yeah, it does speak for itself.

00:10:03.368 --> 00:10:04.836
-It is -- Here.

00:10:04.936 --> 00:10:07.206
-Oh, look at that. Beautiful.

00:10:07.306 --> 00:10:10.175
And, now, I heard that,
historically,

00:10:10.275 --> 00:10:13.312
your connection to the world of
fragrance, of perfume --

00:10:13.412 --> 00:10:14.947
this was something
you shared with your father.

00:10:15.047 --> 00:10:16.549
-Yes.
-And how were you introduced?

00:10:16.649 --> 00:10:19.285
Did he take you to the fanciest
perfume shop in the land?

00:10:19.385 --> 00:10:22.487
-Macy's.
Yeah, he took me to Macy's.

00:10:22.587 --> 00:10:25.591
Yeah, fragrance and, honestly,
just, like, scent

00:10:25.691 --> 00:10:29.061
besides actual fragrances
has been, like, my biggest --

00:10:29.161 --> 00:10:31.029
like, one of my --
the biggest parts of my life,

00:10:31.129 --> 00:10:33.232
if that even makes sense.

00:10:33.332 --> 00:10:35.734
I have what I used to call
a super-sniffer.

00:10:35.835 --> 00:10:37.236
You guys watch "Psyche"
when you were little?

00:10:37.336 --> 00:10:40.439
Because I love that show.
-I watched it when I was little.

00:10:40.539 --> 00:10:47.746
[ laughter ]

00:10:47.846 --> 00:10:48.947
-I want to know --

00:10:49.047 --> 00:10:50.649
-Did you ever watch
"Welcome Back, Kotter"?

00:10:50.749 --> 00:10:55.854
-No.
-Yeah, I thought.

00:10:55.954 --> 00:11:00.326
-No, but I've always had
a severe connection to scent,

00:11:00.426 --> 00:11:01.893
and I remember
everything through scents

00:11:01.993 --> 00:11:04.696
and people and colors
and all this crazy stuff.

00:11:04.796 --> 00:11:08.867
And, like, before I even had
any sort of a career

00:11:08.967 --> 00:11:11.871
or the idea of a career,
fragrance was, like,

00:11:11.971 --> 00:11:13.272
the most important thing
in my life,

00:11:13.372 --> 00:11:15.474
and it, still to this day,
has been.

00:11:15.574 --> 00:11:19.879
And I honestly am,
like, using this, like, facade

00:11:19.979 --> 00:11:21.347
that I've created of
Billie Eilish

00:11:21.447 --> 00:11:22.515
to promote a fragrance.

00:11:22.615 --> 00:11:24.816
Like, I'm, like --
I'm using my own clout.

00:11:24.916 --> 00:11:28.620
-Well, I think everything
you're using your clout for

00:11:28.720 --> 00:11:29.755
is making things better,
not worse.

00:11:29.855 --> 00:11:30.889
And thank you so much
for being here.

00:11:30.989 --> 00:11:32.391
It was such a delight.
-I'm so honored!

00:11:32.491 --> 00:11:34.624
-You guys, that's Billie Eilish.

